{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And we will return</b>: Some say: how did Abraham say 'and we will return'? And others answered that he intended to bring back his bones, and Abraham dismissed them with words so that they would not leave before his return, and so that Isaac would not perceive and flee. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said that Isaac was thirty-seven years old when he was bound, and if these are words of tradition, we accept them; but by way of reasoning, this is not correct, for the righteousness of Isaac ought to have been made known, and his reward should have been double that of his father, since he surrendered himself willingly to slaughter — yet Scripture says nothing about Isaac. And others said that he was five years old; this too is impossible, because he carried the wood for the burnt offering. And the near to reason is that he was close to thirteen years, and his father overpowered him and bound him without his consent. The proof is that his father concealed the secret from him and said, 'Elohim will provide for Himself the lamb' — for had he told him, 'You are the burnt offering,' he might have fled."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "Resolved רז\"ל as 'our Rabbis, of blessed memory' per standard rabbinic abbreviation."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:45:25.004792001Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}